  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some showers from the Southern Tier northeastward \ninto the North Country this morning, then turning drier through the \nrest of the day.\n\nAs of this writing a wave of low pressure is found over the central \nMississippi Valley...with an inverted trough extending northeastward \nfrom this feature into western New York. Regional radars show that \nthe vast majority of any showers attendant to the trough continue to \npass by largely to our south...with just some rather spotty activity \ncurrently noted across the western Southern Tier. \n\nAs we push through the early portions of this morning...the trough \naxis will temporarily extend further northeastward across New York \nState...with an embedded weak wave of low pressure forming over NE \nOhio/NW Pennsylvania in response to shortwave energy pushing \neastward across the Ohio Valley. At the same time...a modest low \nlevel jet will also develop from north-central PA into north-central \nNew York. As all this occurs...increasing isentropic upglide/ \nmoisture along the advancing trough will combine with low level \nconvergence along the nose of the low level jet...leading to a brief \nperiod of somewhat more numerous showers from the interior Southern \nTier northeastward across portions of the Finger Lakes and North \nCountry. These should be most numerous across the North Country \nwhere lift from the LLJ will be maximized...while across the Niagara \nFrontier shower coverage should remain much more widely scattered \ndue to that area lying to the northwest of the better forcing. \n\nAfter about mid-morning the low level jet will slide off to our \neast...while the axis of the inverted surface trough will get \nsuppressed southeastward in response to strong Canadian high \npressure building southeastward across Ontario Province and the \nUpper Great Lakes. This will result in any showers commensurately \nwinding down from northwest to southeast...allowing for a return to \nmainly dry weather by late afternoon in most areas. This being \nsaid...a general northerly/northeasterly upslope flow will keep \nmostly cloudy skies intact across the area for the bulk of the \nday...with just some limited sun possible across northern sections \nlate. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below normal temperatures will continue through most \nof the upcoming week, with a few showers possible at times midweek.\n\nThrough the work week the bulk of broad upper level troughing over \neastern Canada will slowly drift eastward and out to sea...with the \nsouthwestern extent of this trough eventually breaking off and \nevolving into a modest closed upper low over the Ohio Valley/mid-\nAtlantic states during the mid-late week period. Meanwhile at the \nsurface...a large area of cool Canadian high pressure will slowly \ndrift from Ontario to Quebec Provinces. The prevailing northeasterly \nto easterly flow around the high will keep a cooler than normal \nairmass intact across our region through the majority of the \nupcoming week...with the coolest days coming Monday-Tuesday when \nhighs will range from the upper 50s to lower 60s in most areas... \nwith only gradual day-to-day warming then following for the rest of \nthe week.  \n\nWith respect to precipitation chances...uncertainty persists with \nrespect to how far back north inverted troughing extends during the \nmidweek period in response to the developing closed upper low. \nDepending upon just how far north this ultimately gets...there could \nbe a few spotty showers across portions of western New York Tuesday \nthrough Thursday...for which current blended guidance of slight \nchance to chance PoPs looks reasonable.",
